---
# DID THE SITE STOP KNOWING WHICH FILM A LISTING IS? -- the live half of "something stopped being
# matched / changed match". The code-caused half is CI's (the identity takes check in every
# convergence leg resolves the pinned recording at the base and at the head and fails on a moved
# take nobody accepted); this file watches what production actually did, whatever moved it --
# new data, an answer store that changed, an agreement stage taking or withdrawing a film, or a
# deploy.
#
# THE COUNTER: `kinowo_worker_identity_take_changes_total{country,kind}`, written by every identity
# projection that writes (services.identity.TakeChange): a listing STILL PUBLISHED whose TMDB film
# moved -- `lost` (had one, has none), `changed` (another film), `gained` (had none). Expected churn
# is never counted: a listing leaving the schedule has no cluster, a new one no previous film, and a
# re-key or merge that keeps the TMDB film keeps the take. Each change is also logged one line
# (`identity take change: <kind> <venue> | <listing>: <old> -> <new>; reason <basis: why>; ...`), its
# facts also FIELDS (kind, basis, venue, tmdb_before/after, previous_basis, rules, runner_up, repro,
# build, projection_id), and a per-tick `identity take change: summary` line, so the drill-down is
# `country:<cc> kind:lost | stats by (basis) count()` -- see the identity-investigation skill.
#
# WHY A SHARE OF THE COUNTRY'S LISTINGS AND NOT A COUNT. Countries differ 20x in size (ES 5,178
# listings, US 101,678 on 2026-10-13) and in listings per film (PL 9, US 48): one US wide release
# re-matched is ~500 listings, one PL film ~9. A count either pages on every US re-match or never on
# PL. The share asks the same question everywhere: did a visible slice of what the country serves
# change film within the hour?
groups:
  - name: identity-matching
    rules:
      # MORE THAN 0.15% OF A COUNTRY'S LISTINGS LOST THEIR FILM WITHIN AN HOUR (and over 25 of them), held 5 minutes.
      #
      # THE CASE IT EXISTS FOR: build 9c8170f5e, deployed 2026-10-14 04:16Z, and its first UK projection at
      # 04:24Z unmatched 213 listings at once (0.58% of 36,700); the next one, 06:36Z, another 82 (0.23%). The
      # 2%-of-lost-or-changed burst that stood here before never came within a tenth of either.
      #
      # LOST ALONE, because it is the failure that can sit at a fraction of a percent and still be a regression
      # (a listing with no film shows no rating, poster or synopsis), while its everyday level is near zero:
      # the counter's first 18 hours live (2026-10-13 13:25Z - 10-14 07:50Z) peaked per country at PL 0.068%
      # (7 listings), DE 0.058% (20), UK 0.038% (14), US 0.001%, ES 0 -- outside the two bad UK projections.
      # 0.15% sits 2.2x above the highest of them and 1.5x below the smaller UK loss. Changed takes have a
      # benign burst at this scale -- DE re-matched 39 listings (0.12%) at 06:23Z, one film's legitimate
      # re-take -- so lost + changed under one threshold low enough for the UK case would sit within 25% of a
      # healthy hour; changed has its own looser alert below.
      #
      # WHY NOT "AFTER A DEPLOY": worker pods start ~hourly (113 starts across the five countries in the 31h
      # to 2026-10-14 08:00Z), so every hour is "after a deploy" and a deploy gate would filter nothing. The
      # deploy is named by the drill-down instead: each `identity take change` log line carries the `build`
      # that made it, and kube_pod_container_info's `image` tag the build running.
      #
      # The `> 25` floor keeps a small country's handful from paging: 25 is 0.46% of Spain's 5,400 listings.
      # `increase(...[1h])` and `max_over_time` ride out a worker restart's scrape gap (the restart case in
      # the test pins it).
      - alert: IdentityTakesLost
        expr: |
          (
            sum by (country) (increase(kinowo_worker_identity_take_changes_total{kind="lost"}[1h]))
              /
            max by (country) (max_over_time(kinowo_worker_identity_cutover_listings[1h]))
          ) > 0.0015
          and
          sum by (country) (increase(kinowo_worker_identity_take_changes_total{kind="lost"}[1h])) > 25
        for: 5m
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "{{ $labels.country }}: {{ $value | humanizePercentage }} of its listings lost their film within an hour"
          description: >-
            The identity projection unmatched more than 0.15% of this country's published listings in the
            last hour: they had a TMDB film and now have none, so they show no rating, poster or synopsis.
            Its normal hour is under 0.07%. Which ones, why, and which build: VictoriaLogs
            `country:<cc> "identity take change" kind:lost | stats by (build, basis) count()`, one line per
            listing with the resolver's reason. A loss at a worker's first projection after a deploy is
            that build's resolver change -- CI's identity takes check should have listed it; one mid-pod is
            the data or an answer store moving.

      # MORE THAN 0.5% OF A COUNTRY'S LISTINGS RE-MATCHED TO ANOTHER FILM WITHIN AN HOUR (and over 50), held 15m.
      #
      # Lost takes have their own alert above; this is the other half. Its healthy ceiling over the counter's
      # first 18 hours was DE's 0.12% (39 listings re-taken at 06:23Z on 2026-10-14, one film's legitimate
      # move); UK 0.03%, ES 0.02%, PL 0.01%, US 0.005%. 0.5% is 4x above it: a re-match of that size is a
      # whole family of films changing hands (a wide release in the US is ~500 listings, 0.5%). It was 2%
      # of lost and changed together until 2026-10-14, when it missed the UK losses above by 4x.

      - alert: IdentityTakeChangesBurst
        expr: |
          (
            sum by (country) (increase(kinowo_worker_identity_take_changes_total{kind="changed"}[1h]))
              /
            max by (country) (max_over_time(kinowo_worker_identity_cutover_listings[1h]))
          ) > 0.005
          and
          sum by (country) (increase(kinowo_worker_identity_take_changes_total{kind="changed"}[1h])) > 50
        for: 15m
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "{{ $labels.country }}: {{ $value | humanizePercentage }} of its listings changed their film within an hour"
          description: >-
            The identity projection moved more than 0.5% of this country's published listings to another
            TMDB film in the last hour. Listings leaving the schedule and re-keys that keep the film are not
            counted, so this is the site showing different films under the same listings. Which ones and
            why: VictoriaLogs `country:<cc> "identity take change" kind:changed` (one line per listing: venue,
            listing, old -> new film, the resolver's reason, the build). A burst right after a worker deploy
            is the deploy's resolver change -- CI's identity takes check should have listed it; one with no
            deploy is the data or an answer store moving (the agreement stage, a TMDB answer re-read).
            Panel: worker diagnostics, "Identity cutover -- regroupings".

      # MORE THAN 10 SUSPECT TAKES, AND 5%, ABOVE THE SAME TIME YESTERDAY, held for 2 hours.
      #
      # `kinowo_worker_identity_suspect_takes{signal}` (SuspectTakeCount, f6d8cdac3): the billings whose take a
      # suspect-take signal (fragment, era, runtime, sequel, feed id, fill route) finds against its own evidence,
      # set by a whole-corpus projection whose decisions or listings moved. The level is the corpus's standing
      # doubt; a RISE over a day is new wrong takes landing. The convergence legs' suspect-take ratchet names
      # code-caused ones; this sees the rest (new data, an answer store moving).
      #
      # A GAUGE SET ONLY NOW AND THEN, and absent for ~3 minutes after every worker restart until the next whole
      # projection counts again: both sides read it through `last_over_time(...[3h])`, which bridges both (the
      # restart case in the test pins it), and a country with no value a day ago compares against nothing and
      # stays quiet.
      #
      # THE LEVELS, live on 2026-10-13 15:00Z (the gauge's first hours): DE 222, UK 101, PL 106, US 13, ES 2. The
      # proposed +10 alone is 4.5% of Germany's standing count, inside what a week's new programmes can add with no
      # wrong take among them; the 5% beside it keeps the +10 floor for the small countries and asks DE/UK for a
      # rise their size makes notable.
      #
      # CHECKED 2026-10-14 against the gauge's first 17 hours (it was first set ~2026-10-13 15:00Z, so the
      # `offset 1d` side had nothing yet and the expression has never had an input to be true on): the largest
      # 12-hour RISE in any country was +1 (PL); the moves were falls -- DE 218 -> 175 (-43), PL -5, UK -4, US and
      # ES flat. The +10 / 5% bar sits 10x above the observed rise, so it stays; re-check once a week of
      # day-over-day spread exists. The UK loss of 213 takes at 04:24Z moved UK's suspect count by 0: a lost take
      # is no take to doubt, which is why IdentityTakesLost watches it directly.
      - alert: IdentitySuspectTakesRising
        expr: |
          (
            sum by (country) (last_over_time(kinowo_worker_identity_suspect_takes[3h]))
              -
            sum by (country) (last_over_time(kinowo_worker_identity_suspect_takes[3h] offset 1d))
          ) > 10
          and
          (
            sum by (country) (last_over_time(kinowo_worker_identity_suspect_takes[3h]))
              /
            sum by (country) (last_over_time(kinowo_worker_identity_suspect_takes[3h] offset 1d))
          ) > 1.05
        for: 2h
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "{{ $labels.country }}: {{ $value }} more suspect takes than a day ago"
          description: >-
            The live resolve's suspect takes (billings whose film a signal doubts against the listing's own
            evidence: a title fragment, an era, a runtime, a sequel number, a feed id, a fill route) rose by more
            than 10 over the same time yesterday, for two hours. Which signal: worker diagnostics, the identity
            cutover panel (`kinowo_worker_identity_suspect_takes` by signal); which takes: the suspect-take
            audit (scripts.SuspectTakesMeasure over a recording, or the convergence legs' step summary on the next push). A rise
            right after a worker deploy is the deploy's; IdentityTakeChangesBurst or IdentityTakesLost firing beside
            it says the films themselves moved.

      # A LISTING MOVED BACK TO THE FILM IT HELD, IN TWO OR MORE OF THE LAST SIX HOURS.
      #
      # `kinowo_worker_identity_take_reversals_total` (services.identity.TakeReversals): a take change undoing one
      # at most three projections earlier -- two rules taking a listing from each other. UK Chichester's "The Ties
      # That Bind Us" flipped between the 2008 and the 2025 film on every pod start of 2026-10-13/14 (18 reversals
      # in 41 h of take-change logs): the periodic projection's exact-top-hit own match against the changed-scope
      # projection's families-and-poster correction. One reversal in one hour is a quick correction; reversals in
      # two separate hours of six are the same disagreement coming back. Counted hour by hour, so one projection
      # undoing a batch of listings at once is one hour, never two. A counter, read through increase(): a worker
      # restart resets it and increase() bridges that, so no hold is needed.
      - alert: IdentityTakeOscillating
        expr: |
          count_over_time(
            (sum by (country) (increase(kinowo_worker_identity_take_reversals_total[1h])) > 0)[6h:1h]
          ) >= 2
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "{{ $labels.country }}: listings moved back to a film they held, in {{ $value }} of the last 6 hours"
          description: >-
            The identity projection keeps undoing its own take changes: a listing taken from film A to B is
            taken back to A within three projections, and it happened again in another hour. Two rules disagree
            on the listing and each undoes the other (2026-10-13/14: UK "The Ties That Bind Us", the exact-top-hit
            own match against the families-and-poster correction, once per pod start). Which listings:
            VictoriaLogs `country:<cc> "identity take change" | stats by (venue, listing) count() n | sort by (n desc)`,
            then the listing's lines' basis, reason_last and projection_mode on each side of the flip.
            Panel: worker diagnostics, "Identity cutover -- regroupings".
